The **45°–45°–90°** triangle is half a unit square: both legs are $1$ and the hypotenuse is $\sqrt{2}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,3);
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,0);
\draw (A)--(B) node[midway,left] {$1$};
\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,below] {$1$};
\draw (C)--(A) node[midway,above right] {$\sqrt{2}$};
\coordinate (ra1) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (ra2) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (ra1)--($(ra1)+(ra2)-(B)$)--(ra2);
\pic[draw,"$45^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.7cm,angle eccentricity=1.9] {angle=A--C--B};
\pic[draw,"$45^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=2] {angle=B--A--C};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The **30°–60°–90°** triangle is half an equilateral triangle of side $2$, with $1$ opposite $30^{\circ}$, $\sqrt{3}$ opposite $60^{\circ}$ and hypotenuse $2$.

